**Yes—based on that fuller description, it can count as enterprise-wide operating model redesign.**

The redesign is **not the triage calls themselves**. It is the new governance model you established across 40+ vendors and six business units:

- standardized how exceptions were identified and prioritized;
- created shared accountability across organizational boundaries;
- established decision rights and escalation paths;
- coordinated parties over whom you had no direct authority;
- changed the recurring way the enterprise managed vendor performance;
- produced a measurable outcome: 94% SLA compliance.

That is materially different from merely “facilitating calls” or “sustaining performance.” It aligns with your company’s stated Director criterion **if the governance replaced a fragmented prior model and became the standard operating mechanism**, rather than remaining an informal process dependent entirely on your personal intervention.

The key test is:

> **Did you improve execution inside the existing model, or did you change the model by which 40+ vendors and six business units execute?**

From what you’ve now described, it sounds like the latter.

Your locked bullets obscure that fact. They describe the **visible activities**—triage and alignment—while omitting the Director-level contribution: designing and implementing cross-enterprise governance. A concise committee framing would be:

> “Designed and implemented a new enterprise triage governance model across 40+ vendors and six business units, standardizing accountability, prioritization, decision-making, and multi-hub escalation without direct authority; the model sustained 94% vendor SLA compliance.”

So my direct assessment: **yes, this is legitimate evidence of Director-level operating-model redesign.** Whether it secures promotion is separate: the committee may still weigh duration, business impact, repeatability, and your manager’s “Meets/Senior Manager” rating. But it should not be dismissed merely as operational maintenance. Your strongest argument is that the **94% was the output of a redesigned enterprise operating model**, not the entirety of the accomplishment.
